Kyto's source is published so that it can be READ. People trust Kyto with their
messages, their credentials and their repositories, and "you can check what it
actually does" is a better answer to that trust than a promise. Being readable is
not the same as being free to take, and this licence is deliberately narrower than
an open-source one.

1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   You may view, read, and privately discuss this source code. You may quote
   short excerpts for the purpose of commentary, security review, teaching, or
   reporting a problem, with attribution.

2. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   Without prior written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:

   a. copy, reproduce, or redistribute this software or any substantial portion
      of it, in source or compiled form;
   b. run, host, deploy, or operate this software, or any work derived from it,
      whether or not for a fee;
   c. modify it, or create derivative works from it;
   d. incorporate any part of it into another work;
   e. sublicense, sell, rent, or otherwise transfer any rights in it;
   f. use it to train, fine-tune, evaluate, or otherwise develop a machine
      learning model.

   No licence is granted by implication, estoppel, or otherwise. Making the
   source publicly readable is not a grant of any of the rights withheld above.

3. GORKIE-DERIVED CODE IS STILL MIT

   Kyto began as a fork of gorkie, which was published under the MIT licence.
   Code in this repository that originated in gorkie — including this
   repository's history from before Kyto's rewrite — remains available under
   those MIT terms, which are reproduced in ./LICENSE-gorkie-MIT. This licence
   applies to Kyto's own code and cannot and does not withdraw permissions MIT
   already granted for that code. Section 2 is not a claim over gorkie's work.

